- Describe only what the readers the policy names would notice or care about: what they can now do, what works better for them, and what they have to change. Leave out how a change was built, such as internal names, data formats, algorithms, and refactors. Include a technical detail only when readers need it to understand the value or to act, such as a command to run or a setting to change, and keep even that to the minimum.
- Organize changes under these headings, in the order the policy gives, leaving out any with nothing to say:
  - `## ✨ New Features`: what users can do that they couldn't before.
  - `## ⬆️ Improvements`: better behavior, performance, or usability of something that already existed.
  - `## 🐛 Squashed Bugs`: the trigger, the previous wrong behavior, and the corrected result.
  - `## ⛓️‍💥 Breaking Changes`: who is affected, the old and new behavior, and exact migration steps.
- Give upgrade instructions only for breaking changes, and focus them on what readers must change. Don't repeat general upgrade instructions in every release.
- Give significant changes a `###` heading and a sentence on why they matter. Use bullets for small changes, code blocks for commands, and before-and-after examples where they help.
- End every entry with the pull requests it covers, such as `(#7)`, or `(#7, #9)` when an entry combines several. GitHub links these numbers on the release page.
- Group related commits into one entry, and write for the readers the policy names.
- Under `## Pull Requests`, keep each entry as inventory wrote it, such as `- feat: add login command by @octocat in #7`, and group the entries under `### ✨ Features`, `### 🐛 Bug Fixes`, `### 📖 Documentation`, `### 🤖 CI`, and `### 🧹 Chores`, in that order, leaving out empty groups. Group entries by reading each pull request; a title's prefix is only a hint.
- Follow `## Pull Requests` with `## New Contributors` when inventory lists any, then inventory's closing line.
